"Nature∣ 周沛劼与合作者提出构建蛋白质组虚拟细胞新方法-北京大学定量生物学中心 (https://cqb.pku.edu.cn/info/1065/6363.htm)\nciteturn1135search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: last week;  2026年9月9日，北京大学前沿交叉学科研究院周沛劼研究员与西湖大学、西湖实验室、北京科学智能研究院、深势科技、哈尔滨医科大学等单位合作，在《Nature》发表题为“An operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model” [1] 的研究论文，提出基于NeuralODE框架针对动态蛋白质组的虚拟细胞模型ProteinTalks，为药物响应预测与耐药相关线索发现提供新方法。\n\n# 定量生物学中心\n\n# Nature∣ 周沛劼与合作者提出构建蛋白质组虚拟细胞新方法\n\n2026-09-11 23:20:26\n\n 2026年9月9日，北京大学前沿交叉学科研究院周沛劼研究员与西湖大学、西湖实验室、北京科学智能研究院、深势科技、哈尔滨医科大学等单位合作，在《Nature》发表题为“An operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model” [1] 的研究论文，提出基于NeuralODE框架针对动态蛋白质组的虚拟细胞模型ProteinTalks，为药物响应预测与耐药相关线索发现提供新方法。北京大学前沿交叉研究院周沛劼研究员、北京科学智能研究院/北京大学温翰研究员与西湖大学郭天南教授、西湖实验室朱怡研究员为共同通讯作者。西湖大学孙瑞、钱鎏佳，深势科技李永歌及哈尔滨医科大学刘通为共同第一作者，鄂维南院士为方法开发提供了重要指导。该研究工作得到了国家自然科学基金委“科学计算与机器学习”基础科学中心（基金号12288101）等项目等资助。\n\n 蛋白质是细胞功能的主要执行者，也是许多药物直接作用的对象。药物进入细胞后，信号传递、蛋白网络重塑及细胞死亡或耐药表型的形成，在不同时间尺度上逐步展开。围绕这一动态过程，研究团队构建了大规模时间分辨扰动蛋白质组数据集，并通过ProteinTalks联合学习细胞状态及其在药物作用下的演变轨迹，将分子层面的动态变化与药物效果联系起来。模型由此能够预测单药效果和药物组合协同作用，识别值得进一步研究的耐药相关蛋白，并拓展至患者来源模型中的候选药物筛选。团队进一步通过蛋白表达干预和类器官药敏实验，检验模型提出的线索，展示了动态蛋白质组与AI建模相结合、从药物响应预测走向实验发现的研究路径。\n\n为什么需要动态蛋白质组\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model | Nature (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-11001-9)\nciteturn1135search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: today; Here we generated more than 38 million temporal protein-abundance measurements from systematically perturbed breast cancer cell lines, and developed ProteinTalks, a virtual cell model. ... 7 Interpretation of ProteinTalks model using SHAP values for different drugs.\n\n  * Article\n  * Published: 09 September 2026\n\n# An operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model\n\nNature (2026)\n\n  * 13k Accesses\n\n  * 1 Citations\n\n  * 99 Altmetric\n\n  * Metrics details\n\n## Abstract\n\nArtificial intelligence-empowered virtual cell models represent an emerging approach for in silico drug discovery^{1,2,3}, yet most existing approaches lack large-scale, time-resolved perturbation proteomics data and interpretable frameworks for predicting therapeutic responses. Here we generated more than 38 million temporal protein-abundance measurements from systematically perturbed breast cancer cell lines, and developed ProteinTalks, a virtual cell model. Central to ProteinTalks is the synergy of this large-scale dynamic proteomic resource and the model architecture, enabling a new pretraining framework that learns transferable dynamical latent representations from temporal proteome trajectories. By modelling how proteins respond conditionally to different perturbations, this approach enables the model to function as an operational tool for diverse drug discovery tasks: predicting drug efficacy and synergy, discovering new drug combinations, probing proteins associated with drug resistance, stratifying patient responses and prioritizing drug candidates for patient organoids. It also shows robust transferability, extending beyond cell lines to patient-derived organoids and clinical biopsies, generally achieving higher performance than the selected benchmark implementations under the evaluated protocols. Together, ProteinTalks shows how scalable pretraining of transferable dynamic representations enables operational, dynamics-aware, proteomics-based virtual cell models to advance in silico drug discovery.\n\nFig. 1: Overview of ProteinTalks workflow.\n\nImage\n\nFig. 2: Longitudinal proteomic responses to drug perturbation.\n\nImage\n\nFig. 3: ProteinTalks architecture, training strategy and performance benchmarking.\n\nImage\n\nFig. 4: Exploring the interpretability of the ProteinTalks model.\n\nImage\n\nFig. 5: Assessing the clinical relevance of ProteinTalks.\n\nImage\n\n## Data availability\n\nRaw mass spectrometry proteomics data have been deposited in the ProteomeXchange Consortium available via iProX (IPX0007409000). The PTDS protein matrix and associated resources are available for academic and non-commercial use through the open-access platform db.prottalks.com. Other data used are available from the Kyoto Encyclopedia of Genes and Genomes pathway at https://www.genome.jp/kegg/pathway.html (ref. ^{57}), from Metascape at https://metascape.org/gp/ (ref. ^{58}), from STRING at https://cn.string-db.org/ (ref. ^{59}) and from Ingenuity Pathway Analysis at http://www.ingenuity.com (ref. ^{60}).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew AI Model Predicts How Cancer Cells Respond to Drugs | School of Life Sciences (https://lifesciences.westlake.edu.cn/media/news/20260910/new-AI-model-predicts-how-cancer-cells-respond-to-drugs.html)\nciteturn1135search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; Professor Tiannan Guo from Westlake University and collaborators reported ProteinTalks, a virtual cell model with potential clinical applications that learns the dynamics of proteomic responses to drug perturbations in cancer cells. ... Published online: 9 September 2026. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-11001-9\n\nIn a retrospective cohort of 501 previously untreated patients with TNBC, ProteinTalks-derived scores based on baseline tumor proteomes and planned treatment regimens stratified patients by overall and recurrence-free survival. This provides preliminary support for proteome-based prognostic stratification and requires prospective validation.\n\nIn three TNBC patient-derived organoids, ProteinTalks screened approximately 3,000 compounds and identified candidates to which the organoids showed greater sensitivity than to cisplatin, the standard chemotherapy used as the benchmark in these assays.\n\nProteinTalks is not a digital twin of a cell. It aims at addressing a defined, operational task: using a baseline proteome and a specified drug perturbation to predict how protein states evolve and how those trajectories relate to drug response. Its broader contribution lies in providing a practical framework for linking time-resolved perturbation proteomics to predictive virtual cell modeling. As time-resolved data spanning multiple molecular and biological scales continue to accumulate, the technical approach underlying ProteinTalks may help advance virtual-cell models that more faithfully represent biological processes.\n\nImage: 20260910-inarticle-img2.jpg\n\nImage: 20260910-inarticle-img3.jpg\n\nReference: Sun, R., Qian, L., Li, Y. et al. An operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model. Nature. Published online: 9 September 2026. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-11001-9\n\nLab website: https://www.guomics.com\n\nProteinTalks database: https://db.prottalks.com/--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTiny protein detection using pressure through solid‐state nanopores - Li - 2017 - ELECTROPHORESIS - Wiley Online Library (https://analyticalsciencejournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/elps.201600410)\nciteturn1135search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; State Key Laboratory for Mesoscopic Physics and Electron Microscopy Laboratory, School of Physics, Peking University, Beijing, P. ... State Key Laboratory for Mesoscopic Physics and Electron Microscopy Laboratory, School of Physics, Peking University, Beijing, P. ... https://doi.org/10.1002/elps.201600410Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nDapeng Yu\n\nState Key Laboratory for Mesoscopic Physics and Electron Microscopy Laboratory, School of Physics, Peking University, Beijing, P. R. China\n\nCollaborative Innovation Center of Quantum Matter, Beijing, P. R. China\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nQing Zhao, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nQing Zhao\n\n  * zhaoqing@pku.edu.cn\n\nState Key Laboratory for Mesoscopic Physics and Electron Microscopy Laboratory, School of Physics, Peking University, Beijing, P. R. China\n\nCollaborative Innovation Center of Quantum Matter, Beijing, P. R. China\n\nCorrespondence: Professor Qing Zhao, State Key Laboratory for Mesoscopic Physics and Electron Microscopy Laboratory, School of Physics, Peking University, Beijing 100871, P. R. China\n\nE-mail: zhaoqing@pku.edu.cn\n\nFirst published: 09 January 2017\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/elps.201600410Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\nColour Online: See the article online to view Figs. 1–6 in colour.\n\nLi, J., Hu, R., Li, X., Tong, X., Yu, D. and Zhao, Q. (2017), Tiny protein detection using pressure through solid-state nanopores. ELECTROPHORESIS, 38: 1130-1138. https://doi.org/10.1002/elps.201600410\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA perturbation proteomics-based foundation model for virtual cell construction (https://explore.openaire.eu/search/publication?pid=10.1101%2F2025.02.07.637070)\nciteturn1135search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: today; https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.0... ... Here, we generate a large-scale dataset of over 38 million perturbed protein measurements in breast cancer cell lines and develop a neural ordinary differential equation-based foundation model, namely ProteinTalks. ...   * Peking University\nHere, we generate a large-scale dataset of over 38 million perturbed protein measurements in breast cancer cell lines and develop a neural ordinary differential equation-based foundation model, namely ProteinTalks. During pretraining, ProteinTalks gains a fundamental understanding of cellular protein network dynamics. Our model encodes protein networks and exhibits consistently improved predictive accuracy across various downstream tasks, highlighting its generalization capabilities and adaptability. In cancer cells, ProteinTalks robustly predicts drug efficacy and synergy, identifies novel drug combinations, and, through its interpretability, uncovers resistance-associated proteins. When applied to more complex system, patient-derived tumor xenografts, ProteinTalks predicts potential responses to drugs. Its integration with clinical patient data enhances the prognosis prediction of breast cancer patients. Collectively, we present a foundational model based on proteome dynamics, offering potential for various downstream applications, including drug discovery, and providing a basis for developing virtual cells.\n\nRelated Organizations\n\n  * Westlake University\n\nChina (People's Republic of)\n\n  * State Key Laboratory of Proteomics\n\nChina (People's Republic of)\n\n  * Hangzhou First People's Hospital\n\nChina (People's Republic of)\n\n  * Peking University\n\nChina (People's Republic of)\n\nFields of Science\n\nengineering and technology\n\nmedical engineering\n\nFields of Science\n\nengineering and technology\n\nmedical engineering\n\nRelated to Research communities\n\nCancer Research--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn operational perturbation proteomics-based virtual cell model. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42717098/)\nciteturn1135search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 weeks ago; Crawled: last week;   * DOI: 10.1038/s41586-026-11001-9 ...   * Center for Machine Learning Research, Peking University, Beijing, China. ... Here we generated more than 38 million temporal protein-abundance measurements from systematically perturbed breast cancer cell lines, and developed ProteinTalks, a virtual cell model.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA perturbation proteomics-based foundation model for virtual cell construction | bioRxiv (https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.02.07.637070v2)\nciteturn1135search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Here, we generate a large-scale dataset of over 38 million perturbed protein measurements in breast cancer cell lines and develop a neural ordinary differential equati